<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tukoji Rao Holkar III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tukoji Rao Holkar III was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Indore from 1903 to 1926. Taking full powers in 1911, he presided over the growth of Indore's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> trade and public works. In 1926 he abdicated in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashwant-rao-holkar-ii">Yashwant Rao Holkar II</a> rather than face a British inquiry into the murder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> of a merchant who had sheltered his former courtesan, Mumtaz Begum. He later married an American, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> Miller.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tukoji-rao-holkar-iii.jpg" alt="Tukoji Rao Holkar III" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Lafayette · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tukojirao_III_Maharaja_Holkar_of_Indore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tukoji was born on 26 November 1890, the son of Shivajirao Holkar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Indore, and Maharani Sita Bai. His father abdicated on 31 January 1903, and as the new maharaja was only thirteen a council of regency governed until he came of age. He was educated at the Daly College in Indore and at Dehradun. On 6 November 1911 he was invested with ruling powers, and in the same year he attended the coronation of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As ruler he founded two orders, the Order of Merit of Holkar in February 1914 and the Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahilyabai-holkar">Ahilyabai Holkar</a> on 22 November 1920. In the New Year Honours of 1918 he was made a Knight Grand Commander of the Order of the Star of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>In January 1925 Mumtaz Begum, a courtesan and one of his mistresses, left his harem after what she described as repressive conditions and the death of her daughter, which she blamed on palace nurses. While performing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> she met Abdul Bawla, a 25-year-old Memon businessman, who was threatened several times and told to send her back, and refused. On 12 January a group of assassins attacked the couple at Malabar Hill. Bawla was shot in the head and killed; Mumtaz Begum survived with a wound to the face after British officers who were nearby intervened.</p><p>She testified against nine accused, among them men of the maharaja's household. The court sentenced three men to death and three to life imprisonment. One of the latter, General Anandrao Gangram Phanse of the Indore army, escaped the death penalty after being represented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a>. The court did not formally hold Tukoji responsible, but under British pressure he abdicated on 26 February 1926 and left Indore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his abdication he lived mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He had married three times. His senior wife, Chandravati Gawade, whom he married on 16 March 1895 when both were small children, stayed in Indore and brought up their son, the new maharaja. His second wife, Indira Talcherkar, married in 1913, also remained there and became a patron of historical research bodies such as the Rajwade Historical Research Institute.</p><p>In 1928 he married Nancy Anne Miller, who before the wedding embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, was received into the Dhangar community and took the name Sharmista Devi Holkar; they had four daughters. The Bawla case inspired a film of 1925, Kulin Kanta, and two books. Tukoji died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 21 May 1978, leaving one son and six daughters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tukojirao Holkar III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cotton trade made Indore a commercial city; <b>Tukoji Rao III</b> abdicated in 1926 after a murder scandal.</blockquote><a href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Indore, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.72,75.86&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Indore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6579 1325 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tukoji Rao Holkar III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7029 1595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7029 1595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.72,75.86&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
